Beryllium: child processes close the descriptors they inherit (S-IPC-01)

Purpose: base::LaunchProcess() closes every descriptor a child does not
  need by listing /dev/fd (CloseSuperfluousFds). On FreeBSD /dev/fd lists
  only 0, 1 and 2 unless fdescfs is mounted, so the walk closed nothing
  and every child process kept everything the browser had open when it
  was launched (the Capsicum oracle's fd.dev_fd_complete probe predicted
  this, plan/12 section 10.1). The Capsicum survey on the owner's machine
  (0.20.3.3, 4 November 2026) showed a renderer holding about 120
  descriptors: directories (/, /usr, /usr/local, /usr/local/etc, opened by
  the browser to watch the policy directories), writable profile databases
  (Sync Data, Local Storage, Site Characteristics, the GPU caches), the
  ktrace and log files and the DRM device. Capability mode does not take
  any of that away: lookups beneath a directory descriptor still work, so
  a confined renderer holding "/" could read the whole file system.
  On FreeBSD the child now keeps 0, 1, 2 and the descriptors the launcher
  remapped for it (fds_to_remap: the Mojo channel, field-trial memory and
  the rest Chromium passes on purpose) and closes everything else, with
  close() below the highest kept descriptor and closefrom(2) above it,
  both async-signal-safe between fork and exec. This is what Chromium does
  on Linux through /proc/self/fd, so no child should depend on an
  inherited descriptor.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported FreeBSD patch set
  only as far as base/process/launch_posix.cc already lists FreeBSD.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py
  test_renderers_hold_no_directories_or_writable_files (renderers hold no
  directory and no writable file beyond 0-2); the Capsicum survey's
  descriptor list per process type.
security-review: required (process launch, descriptor passing)

--- a/base/process/launch_posix.cc
+++ b/base/process/launch_posix.cc
@@ -207,7 +207,7 @@
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_SOLARIS)
 static const char kFDDir[] = "/dev/fd";
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
-static const char kFDDir[] = "/dev/fd";
+// Beryllium: CloseSuperfluousFds() uses closefrom(2) on FreeBSD.
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_OPENBSD)
 static const char kFDDir[] = "/dev/fd";
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
@@ -218,6 +218,35 @@
   // DANGER: no calls to malloc or locks are allowed from now on:
   // http://crbug.com/36678
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+  // Beryllium (feature matrix S-IPC-01): /dev/fd lists only 0, 1 and 2
+  // unless fdescfs is mounted, so the directory walk below closes nothing
+  // and every child inherited the browser's open files: the Capsicum survey
+  // (4 November 2026) found renderers holding ~120 descriptors, among them
+  // directories (/, /usr, /usr/local) and writable profile databases, which
+  // capability mode does not take away. Keep 0-2 and the remapped
+  // descriptors, close everything else; closefrom(2) is async-signal-safe.
+  int highest_kept = STDERR_FILENO;
+  for (size_t j = 0; j < saved_mapping.size(); j++) {
+    if (saved_mapping[j].dest > highest_kept) {
+      highest_kept = saved_mapping[j].dest;
+    }
+  }
+  for (int fd = STDERR_FILENO + 1; fd < highest_kept; ++fd) {
+    // Cannot use STL iterators here, since debug iterators use locks.
+    size_t j;
+    for (j = 0; j < saved_mapping.size(); j++) {
+      if (fd == saved_mapping[j].dest) {
+        break;
+      }
+    }
+    if (j == saved_mapping.size()) {
+      close(fd);
+    }
+  }
+  closefrom(highest_kept + 1);
+  return;
+#else
   // Get the maximum number of FDs possible.
   size_t max_fds = GetMaxFds();
 
@@ -282,6 +311,7 @@
     int ret = IGNORE_EINTR(close(static_cast<int>(fd)));
     DPCHECK(ret == 0);
   }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
 }
 
 Process LaunchProcess(const CommandLine& cmdline,
